// drive.cla: the front-end-neutral compile/emit pipeline, extracted out of
// main.cla's `on App.startCLI` (Task 6, mac-resident-clarusc phase) so a
// future Mac-resident GUI front end (macgui, later tasks in this phase) can
// drive the SAME pipeline without a CLI shell around it. Pure code motion:
// every front end (today: main.cla's host CLI) must see byte-identical
// behavior to before this file existed -- see main.cla's own `feReadSource`
// for the seam a front end plugs into.
//
// A front end drives this file as: set `hostPaths`/`wantEmit`/`want68k`
// (below) for the invocation at hand, call `driveCompile`, and on success
// either read `combined` itself (appinfo-style metadata) or call
// `driveEmit68k` (or, C target, do its own shakeProgram+emitProgram --
// main.cla still owns that fork, see its own startCLI).

// hostPaths: true selects '/' path semantics (main.cla, the host CLI);
// false selects HFS ':' semantics (Task 8: drivePathJoin/driveKeyResolve
// + expand()'s Mac arm) for the future macgui front end (Task 10, which
// sets it false and supplies the resource-backed feReadSource). main.cla
// still sets it true unconditionally, so every hostPaths=false branch
// added below is dead code on host today -- exercised for real only by
// Task 11's on-Mac compile.
var hostPaths: bool

// wantEmit: the front end sets this true, before calling driveCompile,
// exactly when it intends to lower the checked program to IR afterward
// (main.cla's `emit`/`emit68k` subcommands both do). It gates the
// runtime-manifest splice + second checkProgram + lowerProgram stage
// inside driveCompile, reproducing startCLI's original
// `if emitMode or emitMode68k { ... }` gate now that check-only and
// appinfo callers share the same driveCompile entry point. Left at its
// zero value (false) for check-only/appinfo callers: neither one needs,
// or historically ever paid for, a runtime-module resolution.
var wantEmit: bool

// want68k: the front end additionally sets this true for the emit68k
// target specifically (never the C `emit` target) -- gates native.cla's
// manifest entry and the app raw-name/icon-path resolution below, exactly
// mirroring startCLI's original nested `if emitMode68k { ... }`.
var want68k: bool

// combined is the FIRST-pass assembled decl chain: Phase A's post-order
// assembly, after the --testapi early splice (driveEarlySplice) if that
// ran, but BEFORE the runtime-manifest splice (driveManifestSplice).
// Exposed as a global so a front end can still read it once driveCompile
// returns (main.cla's appinfoMode block scans it for the app section,
// exactly as the pre-split code did) and so driveManifestSplice can
// resolve the app's raw name/icon path from the same chain the original
// code read them from.
var combined: int

// combined2 is the manifest-spliced chain lowerProgram actually runs over
// -- combined itself when driveManifestSplice added nothing (or wantEmit
// was never set). Exposed for symmetry with `combined`, though no front
// end needs to read it directly today.
var combined2: int

// rtDir/haveRtDir mirror the CLI's --rtdir flag (moved from local vars to
// globals here since driveEarlySplice/driveManifestSplice both need them
// and driveCompile's own signature has no room for them): the front end
// sets haveRtDir=true and rtDir to the flag's value when given, otherwise
// leaves both at their zero value and findRtDir's own upward search fills
// rtDir in lazily, the first time a runtime module is actually needed.
var rtDir: string
var haveRtDir: bool

// seenPaths keys the include-once set (driver.go's `seen`), keyed by the
// path after normalizePath's lexical cleanup: "//" and "./" segments
// collapsed and "seg/.." pairs resolved, so "sub/common.cla" and
// "sub/../sub/common.cla" are the same identity. Purely lexical -- an
// absolute and a relative spelling of the same file stay distinct by design.
// The VALUE is the file's asmHeads index PLUS ONE once expand has finished
// parsing it, or 0 while its expansion is still in progress (an include
// cycle re-entering it) -- dedupHits below turns that back into an index.
var seenPaths: map of int

// asmHeads holds each expanded file's decl-list head in POST-ORDER
// (dependencies before includers), matching driver.go's files/trees order.
var asmHeads: list of int

// asmTails is parallel to asmHeads: each file's OWN last decl, recorded at
// parse time before any assembly loop links one file's tail to the next
// file's head. lastDecl() answers the same question only while a head is
// still unlinked, so any assembly that re-orders already-linked segments
// (the hoist path below) must use this instead.
var asmTails: list of int

// dedupHits records, in expansion order, the asmHeads index of every file
// whose expansion was skipped because it had already been expanded (-1 for
// a still-in-progress cycle). The runtime-module splices read the slice of
// this list their own expand() call appended: a runtime module that
// include-dedups against a file the USER already composed positionally
// needs that file's decls HOISTED out of the user chain and into the
// runtime chain, ahead of the module that needs them (extern record TYPE
// references are order-sensitive, unlike function references).
var dedupHits: list of int

// hoistedHead flags the asmHeads indices already hoisted out of the user
// chain (one entry per user-side file, filled at the end of Phase A), and
// hoistCount counts them -- a splice that hoisted nothing rebuilds the
// combined chain exactly as it always did, by appending the user chain
// wholesale.
var hoistedHead: list of bool
var hoistCount: int

// userHeadCount is asmHeads.count after Phase A -- indices below it are the
// user program's own files (hoistable), indices at or above it belong to a
// runtime module already spliced into the runtime chain (never hoisted).
var userHeadCount: int

// entryFailed records that an entry path itself could not be read -- Go's
// Expand returns this as a hard error (clarus check: stderr + exit 1). A
// front end that gets `false` back from driveCompile checks this FIRST
// (before rendering `diags`, which stays empty in this case) to reproduce
// the exact "cannot open entry file" message.
var entryFailed: bool

// dirOf returns path's directory including the trailing '/', or "" if path
// has no slash (mirrors filepath.Dir + a following Join for our purposes).
func dirOf(path: string): string {
    var i: int
    var last: int

    last = -1
    i = 0
    while i < path.length {
        if path[i] == '/' {
            last = i
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if last == -1 {
        return ""
    }
    return path[0, last + 1]
}

// baseNameNoExt returns path's final path segment with a trailing ".cla"
// stripped (if present) -- used by appinfo's filename-fallback rung.
func baseNameNoExt(path: string): string {
    var i: int
    var last: int
    var base: string

    last = -1
    i = 0
    while i < path.length {
        if path[i] == '/' {
            last = i
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    base = path[last + 1, path.length - (last + 1)]
    if base.length >= 4 and base[base.length - 4, 4] == ".cla" {
        base = base[0, base.length - 4]
    }
    return base
}

// joinPath resolves an include's path relative to dir (filepath.Join): an
// absolute name (leading '/') wins outright, otherwise it hangs off dir.
func joinPath(dir: string, name: string): string {
    if name.length > 0 and name[0] == '/' {
        return name
    }
    return dir + name
}

// normalizePath lexically cleans a path for the include-once identity
// (seenPaths) and diagnostics: collapses "//" and "./" segments and
// resolves each "seg/.." pair, preserving a leading "/" and any leading
// "../" run. Purely lexical -- no symlink, case, or cwd resolution: an
// absolute and a relative spelling of the same file stay distinct
// (documented in the reference's include section).
func normalizePath(path: string): string {
    var segs: list of string
    var work: string
    var cur: string
    var junk: string
    var out: string
    var i: int
    var abs: bool

    if not hostPaths {
        // Mac: drivePathJoin/driveKeyResolve (below) already fold every
        // "."/".." at construction time -- HFS climbs become extra leading
        // colons, key-space climbs are popped/stripped -- so a disk-form
        // or key-form identity reaching here is already lexically clean.
        // Passthrough; include dedup on Mac is exact-match on this string
        // (HFS case-insensitivity is a documented simplification, design
        // spec section 5).
        return path
    }

    abs = path.length > 0 and path[0] == '/'
    work = path + "/"
    cur = ""
    i = 0
    while i < work.length {
        if work[i] == '/' {
            if cur == ".." {
                if segs.count > 0 and segs[segs.count - 1] != ".." {
                    junk = segs.pop()
                } else if not abs {
                    segs.add("..")
                }
            } else if cur != "" and cur != "." {
                segs.add(cur)
            }
            cur = ""
        } else {
            cur = cur + work[i]
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    out = ""
    if abs {
        out = "/"
    }
    i = 0
    while i < segs.count {
        if i > 0 {
            out = out + "/"
        }
        out = out + segs[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// drivePathJoin resolves an include's relative path against dir. hostPaths:
// today's joinPath, unchanged (an absolute name wins outright, otherwise it
// hangs off dir).
//
// hostPaths=false (Mac, Task 8): rel is always '/'-separated -- design spec
// section 5: "/" is the only separator allowed in source files -- and gets
// converted to HFS ':' form at resolution time. HFS has no ".." name; its
// only "go up" notation is an empty path component (adjacent colons), so
// each LEADING ".." segment in rel becomes one extra colon appended to dir,
// and the remaining segments join with ':'. dir=="" (no directory context
// yet -- e.g. an entry file opened by a bare Standard File name) is treated
// as ":" (current-directory-relative), matching the design spec's own
// standalone conversion examples.
//
// Worked examples (task brief + design spec section 5, verbatim, plus the
// dir=="" and combined-climb cases):
//   drivePathJoin("Disk:src:", "a/b.cla")  == "Disk:src:a:b.cla"
//   drivePathJoin("", "a/b.cla")           == ":a:b.cla"
//   drivePathJoin("", "../x.cla")          == "::x.cla"
//   drivePathJoin("Disk:src:", "../x.cla") == "Disk:src::x.cla"
func drivePathJoin(dir: string, rel: string): string {
    var segs: list of string
    var cur: string
    var climbs: int
    var leadingDone: bool
    var out: string
    var base: string
    var i: int

    if hostPaths {
        return joinPath(dir, rel)
    }

    climbs = 0
    leadingDone = false
    cur = ""
    i = 0
    while i <= rel.length {
        if i == rel.length or rel[i] == '/' {
            if cur == ".." and not leadingDone {
                climbs = climbs + 1
            } else if cur != "" {
                segs.add(cur)
                leadingDone = true
            }
            cur = ""
        } else {
            cur = cur + rel[i]
        }
        i = i + 1
    }

    base = dir
    if base == "" {
        base = ":"
    }
    out = base
    i = 0
    while i < climbs {
        out = out + ":"
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < segs.count {
        if i > 0 {
            out = out + ":"
        }
        out = out + segs[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// driveMacDirOf returns diskPath's directory including the trailing ':',
// mirroring dirOf but for HFS ':'-separated disk-form paths -- used only
// when hostPaths is false, to seed drivePathJoin's `dir` argument for an
// included file's own further includes. "" if diskPath has no ':' (e.g.
// a bare entry-file name with no directory component of its own yet).
func driveMacDirOf(diskPath: string): string {
    var i: int
    var last: int

    last = -1
    i = 0
    while i < diskPath.length {
        if diskPath[i] == ':' {
            last = i
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if last == -1 {
        return ""
    }
    return diskPath[0, last + 1]
}

// driveKeyResolve computes the resource key (pure '/'-space arithmetic --
// no HFS anywhere in a key, by design: a key is a 'CLFS' resource name,
// matching the --bake key convention, never a disk path) for an include
// named rel, relative to the includer's own key baseKey. Uses dirOf
// (unconditionally '/'-based, same as normalizePath's dot-segment cleanup
// below) regardless of hostPaths -- key space is always '/' space, even
// when the driver as a whole is running in HFS disk-path mode, since a
// file's key and its disk path are independent identities threaded in
// parallel by expand(). One difference from normalizePath: a leading
// "../" run that tries to climb above the root is STRIPPED instead of
// kept -- there is no "above the root" in key space, only the flat
// baked-resource namespace a --bake invocation populated.
//
// Worked examples (task brief, verbatim, plus the resource-includer case):
//   driveKeyResolve("", "toolbox/files.cla")               == "toolbox/files.cla"
//   driveKeyResolve("", "../../toolbox/files.cla")         == "toolbox/files.cla"
//   // A file itself loaded FROM a resource carries its key as its own
//   // recorded identity (expand()'s `path`); further includes from such a
//   // file resolve against THAT key -- e.g. a baked "toolbox/files.cla"
//   // including "../osutils.cla":
//   driveKeyResolve("toolbox/files.cla", "../osutils.cla") == "osutils.cla"
//   // Both-ways case (fix round 1, review finding): a disk-based include
//   // chain and a resource-based include chain that both name the same
//   // relative target converge on the IDENTICAL key, so expand() (which
//   // now uses this key, not the disk path, as every non-entry file's
//   // identity on Mac) dedups them against each other instead of parsing
//   // the target twice -- a user file at key "src/a.cla" including
//   // "../../toolbox/files.cla", and a baked "toolbox/osutils.cla"
//   // resource including "files.cla", both land on "toolbox/files.cla":
//   driveKeyResolve("src/a.cla", "../../toolbox/files.cla") == "toolbox/files.cla"
//   driveKeyResolve("toolbox/osutils.cla", "files.cla")     == "toolbox/files.cla"
func driveKeyResolve(baseKey: string, rel: string): string {
    var segs: list of string
    var work: string
    var cur: string
    var junk: string
    var out: string
    var i: int

    work = dirOf(baseKey) + rel + "/"
    cur = ""
    i = 0
    while i < work.length {
        if work[i] == '/' {
            if cur == ".." {
                if segs.count > 0 and segs[segs.count - 1] != ".." {
                    junk = segs.pop()
                }
                // else: this ".." climbs above the root -- stripped, not
                // kept (unlike normalizePath's host arm, which preserves a
                // leading "../" run for a relative host path).
            } else if cur != "" and cur != "." {
                segs.add(cur)
            }
            cur = ""
        } else {
            cur = cur + work[i]
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    out = ""
    i = 0
    while i < segs.count {
        if i > 0 {
            out = out + "/"
        }
        out = out + segs[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// findRtDir resolves the default --rtdir when the flag is omitted: it walks
// UP from the current working directory looking for a "runtime/clarus/"
// directory, probed by trying to read probeFile (the first needed module's
// file name) out of each candidate. Tries the cwd itself plus up to 9 parent
// levels (10 total) and returns the first candidate that has probeFile, or
// "" if none of them do -- absence here is not itself an error; the caller
// only reports one once a needed module truly can't be found.
func findRtDir(probeFile: string): string {
    var prefix: string
    var i: int
    var candidate: string
    var probe: text
    var ok: bool

    prefix = ""
    i = 0
    while i < 10 {
        candidate = prefix + "runtime/clarus/"
        probe = ""
        ok = file.readText(candidate + probeFile, probe)
        if ok {
            return candidate
        }
        prefix = prefix + "../"
        i = i + 1
    }
    return ""
}

// lastDecl returns the last node in head's `next` chain (head must be a real
// index, not -1) -- used to splice one file's decl list onto the running
// combined list.
func lastDecl(head: int): int {
    var d: int

    d = head
    while declNext(d) != -1 {
        d = declNext(d)
    }
    return d
}

// expand loads, lexes, and parses path, recursing into its leading includes
// first (post-order decl assembly into asmHeads, pre-order parse
// diagnostics) -- a port of driver.go's inner `expand`. Returns whether path
// itself could not be read (the caller turns a missing non-entry file into a
// "cannot open included file" diagnostic at the include's position).
//
// The source read itself goes through the front end's feReadSource seam
// (Task 6) rather than a direct file.readText. `key` (a parameter as of
// Task 8, was a purely-local best-effort hint before) is the RESOLVED
// resource key for rawPath itself, computed by the CALLER before this call:
// driveCompile's entry loop passes "" (an entry file has no key -- design
// spec: the compile model is default-directory-relative, no baked
// counterpart), and this function's own include loop below passes
// driveKeyResolve(<this file's own key>, incName) for each include.
//
// hostPaths=true (host): `key` is IGNORED and recomputed exactly as before
// Task 8 -- path's tail past rtDir when path resolves inside the current
// runtime-module directory, "" otherwise -- so main.cla's front end (which
// itself ignores key entirely) sees byte-identical behavior.
//
// hostPaths=false (Mac, Task 8; identity rule fixed TWICE in review --
// round 1 closed a false-miss, round 2 closed the false-merge round 1's
// own fix introduced): rawPath is the HFS disk-form path a caller's
// drivePathJoin already built, and it's always what's threaded to
// feReadSource and to driveMacDirOf/drivePathJoin for the next level --
// design spec section 4's filesystem-first readSource() check runs
// unconditionally, regardless of how the includer itself was found.
//
// The RECORDED identity (seenPaths dedup key(s), diagnostics, pathIdx) is
// picked as follows:
//   - entry file: always its disk path. Standard File never hands back a
//     resource, so an entry is always a real disk file, and mirroring
//     host here matters -- two distinct entries must stay distinct even
//     though both are handed key="" (driveCompile: `expand(entries[i],
//     true, "")`).
//   - non-entry file, feHasKey(key) false: a plain disk file whose key
//     could NEVER be satisfied by a resource (the common case -- most
//     Mac source isn't baked). Disk-path identity, exactly like an entry.
//     Round-1's mistake was using `key` here unconditionally: two
//     UNRELATED disk files that happen to share a relative-name
//     composition (two independent entries each including a sibling
//     "helper.cla", or one entry including both "a.cla" and "../a.cla" --
//     the latter's leading ".." strips past driveKeyResolve's root, per
//     its own doc comment, landing on the same key "a.cla" as the
//     former) would silently collide and dedup away a genuinely distinct
//     file, with no diagnostic. feHasKey gates that off: neither example
//     names a baked resource, so both stay on disk-path identity and
//     never touch key-space at all -- no collision.
//   - non-entry file, feHasKey(key) true: a key a resource COULD satisfy
//     (the curated --bake namespace, e.g. "toolbox/files.cla"). Probe the
//     filesystem directly (file.readText -- a second read of a file
//     feReadSource is about to read again, same "probe before expand"
//     idiom this file already uses for runtime modules in
//     driveEarlySplice/driveManifestSplice): a disk hit registers BOTH
//     the disk path (primary/display identity -- diagnostics still show
//     the real, openable location) AND the key (a same-entry alias, so a
//     LATER include of the identical logical file that reaches it via a
//     resource-loaded includer's key-space chain still dedups against
//     it, closing round 1's original both-ways gap); a disk miss means
//     only the resource can satisfy it, so the key alone is the identity
//     (design spec's own resource-loaded-file rule, unchanged from
//     round 1).
// feHasKey (a new front-end seam function, alongside feReadSource) is
// main.cla's own "always false" (host has no baked resources); Task 10's
// macgui implements it for real (a resource-presence check, e.g.
// Get1NamedResource + immediate ReleaseResource against 'CLFS').
//
// Worked examples of what does NOT merge (round 2's fix -- both
// unreachable via feHasKey, whose real answer only Task 10 can give, so
// both stay on disk-path identity and dedup exactly like host does today):
//   Two independent entries "DirA:main.cla" and "DirB:other.cla", each
//   including sibling "helper.cla". Pre-round-2 (key-always): both compute
//   driveKeyResolve("", "helper.cla") == "helper.cla" -- same string,
//   second one silently dropped. Post-round-2: feHasKey("helper.cla") is
//   false (nobody bakes a resource literally named "helper.cla"), so both
//   take the entry/no-key branch -- path is drivePathJoin-built from each
//   include's OWN dir, "DirA:helper.cla" vs "DirB:helper.cla" -- distinct,
//   both parsed.
//   One entry "Proj:main.cla" including both "a.cla" and "../a.cla".
//   Pre-round-2: driveKeyResolve("", "a.cla") == "a.cla" and
//   driveKeyResolve("", "../a.cla") == "a.cla" too (the leading ".." has
//   nothing to pop at the root, so it's stripped per driveKeyResolve's own
//   past-root rule) -- same key, second one silently dropped, even though
//   "Proj:a.cla" and the parent directory's "a.cla" are genuinely different
//   files. Post-round-2: same feHasKey("a.cla") == false gate -- both stay
//   on disk-path identity ("Proj:a.cla" vs "Proj::a.cla", drivePathJoin's
//   HFS parent-climb form) -- distinct, both parsed.
func expand(rawPath: string, entry: bool, key: string): bool {
    var path: string
    var altPath: string
    var readPath: string
    var src: text
    var ok: bool
    var head: int
    var dir: string
    var d: int
    var pathIdx: int
    var incName: string
    var incResolved: string
    var incKey: string
    var missing: bool
    var diskProbe: text

    altPath = ""
    if hostPaths {
        readPath = normalizePath(rawPath)
        path = readPath
        key = ""
        if rtDir != "" and readPath.length > rtDir.length and readPath[0, rtDir.length] == rtDir {
            key = readPath[rtDir.length, readPath.length - rtDir.length]
        }
    } else if entry or not feHasKey(key) {
        // Entry file, or a non-entry file whose key no resource could ever
        // satisfy: plain disk-path identity (see the function comment above
        // for why this must NOT fall through to key-as-identity).
        readPath = rawPath
        path = readPath
    } else {
        // Non-entry, feHasKey(key) true: could be satisfied either way.
        readPath = rawPath
        diskProbe = ""
        if file.readText(readPath, diskProbe) {
            path = readPath
            altPath = key
        } else {
            path = key
        }
    }

    if seenPaths.has(path) {
        // Record WHICH file this include deduped against (index, or -1 for
        // a file still being expanded further up the recursion), so a
        // runtime-module splice can hoist a user-composed file it needs.
        dedupHits.add(seenPaths[path] - 1)
        return false
    }
    if altPath != "" and seenPaths.has(altPath) {
        dedupHits.add(seenPaths[altPath] - 1)
        return false
    }
    seenPaths[path] = 0
    if altPath != "" {
        seenPaths[altPath] = 0
    }

    src = ""
    ok = feReadSource(readPath, key, src)
    if not ok {
        if entry {
            entryFailed = true
        }
        return true
    }

    // Stamp every lex/parse diagnostic this file produces with its own path
    // (curPathIdx is read by emitDiag). Set it BEFORE lexing/parsing.
    pathIdx = intern(path)
    curPathIdx = pathIdx
    lexAll(src)
    head = parseFile()

    // Record each top-level decl's owning file so the single check pass can
    // attribute a check diagnostic to the file its decl came from (Go's
    // per-decl c.f), not the entry path.
    d = head
    while d != -1 {
        setDeclFile(d, pathIdx)
        d = declNext(d)
    }

    if hostPaths {
        dir = dirOf(readPath)
    } else {
        dir = driveMacDirOf(readPath)
    }
    d = head
    while d != -1 {
        if declKind(d) == DkInclude {
            incName = poolGet(includePath(d))
            if not hostPaths and incName.length > 0 and incName[0] == '/' {
                emitDiag(declLine(d), declCol(d), "absolute include paths are not supported on Mac")
            } else {
                incResolved = drivePathJoin(dir, incName)
                incKey = ""
                if not hostPaths {
                    incKey = driveKeyResolve(key, incName)
                }
                missing = expand(incResolved, false, incKey)
                if missing {
                    // Recursion may have moved curPathIdx into a child file;
                    // restore this file's path before stamping the diagnostic,
                    // which Go attributes to the includer.
                    curPathIdx = pathIdx
                    emitDiag(declLine(d), declCol(d), "cannot open included file \"" + incName + "\"")
                }
            }
        }
        d = declNext(d)
    }

    asmHeads.add(head)
    if head == -1 {
        asmTails.add(-1)
    } else {
        asmTails.add(lastDecl(head))
    }
    seenPaths[path] = asmHeads.count
    if altPath != "" {
        seenPaths[altPath] = asmHeads.count
    }
    return false
}

// hoistDedups returns the asmHeads INDICES of the USER-side files that the
// dedupHits entries from index fromHit onward (i.e. the ones a single
// runtime-module expand() call just appended) resolved to, marking each
// hoisted so it is skipped when the user chain is rebuilt and never hoisted
// twice. Hits on runtime-side files are ignored: those are already in the
// runtime chain, ahead of the module that deduped against them.
func hoistDedups(fromHit: int): list of int {
    var out: list of int
    var j: int
    var hit: int

    j = fromHit
    while j < dedupHits.count {
        hit = dedupHits[j]
        if hit >= 0 and hit < userHeadCount and not hoistedHead[hit] {
            hoistedHead[hit] = true
            hoistCount = hoistCount + 1
            if asmHeads[hit] != -1 {
                out.add(hit)
            }
        }
        j = j + 1
    }
    return out
}

// driveEarlySplice implements the --testapi early splice (ui-scenario-
// retirement Task 2, ported verbatim off main.cla's old startCLI): a
// program calling any `UiTest*` name would otherwise fail the ordinary
// "clean standalone" gate just below outright, since UiTestClick and
// friends are ordinary top-level funcs declared only in uitest.cla --
// `testapi` opts a UI program (any window/menu/every decl) into splicing
// core/str/text/list/map + the ui*.cla set + uitest.cla into `combined`
// BEFORE driveCompile's own first checkProgram call, so the ONE call
// resolves every UiTest* name at once. A non-UI program, or testapi=false,
// is unaffected either way -- returns false and leaves `combined` alone.
// Returns whether it spliced (earlySpliceDone): driveManifestSplice must
// not re-add the same module set when it did.
func driveEarlySplice(testapi: bool): bool {
    var earlyIsUiProg: bool
    var earlyMods: list of string
    var earlyRtHeads: list of int
    var earlyTail: int
    var earlyCombined2: int
    var d: int
    var i: int
    var j: int
    var rtPath: string
    var rtProbe: text
    var rtReadable: bool
    var beforeCount: int
    var beforeHits: int
    var hoists: list of int
    var h: int

    if not testapi or combined == -1 {
        return false
    }
    earlyIsUiProg = false
    d = combined
    while d != -1 {
        if declKind(d) == DkWindow or declKind(d) == DkMenu or declKind(d) == DkEvery {
            earlyIsUiProg = true
        }
        d = declNext(d)
    }
    if not earlyIsUiProg {
        return false
    }

    earlyMods.add("core.cla")
    earlyMods.add("str.cla")
    earlyMods.add("text.cla")
    earlyMods.add("list.cla")
    earlyMods.add("map.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uidesc.cla")
    earlyMods.add("ui.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uiwidgets.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uitext.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uitable.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uiscript.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uidialogs.cla")
    earlyMods.add("uitest.cla")
    cpCorePorted = true
    cpStrPorted = true
    cpTextPorted = true
    cpTextArcPorted = true
    cpListPorted = true
    cpListArcPorted = true
    cpMapPorted = true
    cpMapArcPorted = true

    if not haveRtDir {
        rtDir = findRtDir(earlyMods[0])
    }
    i = 0
    while i < earlyMods.count {
        rtPath = joinPath(rtDir, earlyMods[i])
        rtProbe = ""
        rtReadable = file.readText(rtPath, rtProbe)
        if not rtReadable {
            if haveRtDir {
                log("runtime module " + earlyMods[i] + " not found (searched " + rtDir + "); use --rtdir")
            } else {
                log("runtime module " + earlyMods[i] + " not found (searched runtime/clarus/ from the working directory upward); use --rtdir")
            }
            quit 1
        }
        // Same whole-range capture as driveManifestSplice's loop below -- a
        // multi-file runtime module (one that itself `include`s another)
        // pushes more than one asmHeads entry per expand call. Files this
        // module's includes deduped against (a file the user already
        // composed positionally) push no asmHeads entry at all, so they are
        // hoisted separately, AHEAD of this module's own heads (post-order,
        // exactly where a freshly expanded include would have landed).
        beforeCount = asmHeads.count
        beforeHits = dedupHits.count
        expand(rtPath, true, "")
        hoists = hoistDedups(beforeHits)
        j = 0
        while j < hoists.count {
            earlyRtHeads.add(hoists[j])
            j = j + 1
        }
        j = beforeCount
        while j < asmHeads.count {
            if asmHeads[j] != -1 {
                earlyRtHeads.add(j)
            }
            j = j + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }

    // Rebuild `combined` with the early runtime-module heads FIRST
    // (declare-before-use), followed by the original user-program chain.
    // earlyRtHeads holds asmHeads INDICES (not heads), so each segment's
    // own end comes from asmTails -- lastDecl would run off the end of a
    // HOISTED segment, whose tail Phase A already linked to the next user
    // file's head.
    earlyTail = -1
    earlyCombined2 = -1
    i = 0
    while i < earlyRtHeads.count {
        h = asmHeads[earlyRtHeads[i]]
        if earlyCombined2 == -1 {
            earlyCombined2 = h
        } else {
            earlyTail = declListAppend(earlyTail, h)
        }
        earlyTail = asmTails[earlyRtHeads[i]]
        i = i + 1
    }
    if earlyCombined2 == -1 {
        earlyCombined2 = combined
    } else if hoistCount == 0 {
        earlyTail = declListAppend(earlyTail, combined)
    } else {
        // Hoisting happened: the user chain has to be re-linked segment by
        // segment, skipping the hoisted files (now in the runtime chain
        // above) and terminating explicitly -- the last kept segment's
        // `next` still points at whatever Phase A linked after it, which
        // may be a hoisted file.
        i = 0
        while i < userHeadCount {
            h = asmHeads[i]
            if h != -1 and not hoistedHead[i] {
                earlyTail = declListAppend(earlyTail, h)
                earlyTail = asmTails[i]
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        earlyTail = declListAppend(earlyTail, -1)
    }
    combined = earlyCombined2
    return true
}

// driveManifestSplice implements the post-check runtime-module manifest
// (ported verbatim off main.cla's old startCLI, split out only for
// function-size headroom): core/str/text/list/map unconditionally unless
// driveEarlySplice already spliced them, ui*.cla when the program is a UI
// program, ser.cla when it uses file save/load, native.cla for a 68k
// target (want68k) -- then rebuilds `combined2` with the runtime chain
// first (declare-before-use) and re-checks it. want68k also resolves the
// app's raw name/icon path into cg68k.cla's cgAppRawName/cgAppIconPath
// globals here (Task 13, native-5e resource parity's original spot),
// since cg68Program (driveEmit68k) needs both and neither reaches IR.
// `entries` is driveCompile's own entries param, threaded through only for
// the app-name fallback's baseNameNoExt(entries[0]).
//
// Sets the global combined2. Returns false on any new diagnostic (the
// front end renders `diags` itself, exactly as driveCompile's own doc
// comment promises).
func driveManifestSplice(entries: list of string, earlySpliceDone: bool): bool {
    var isUiProg: bool
    var d: int
    var appIdx: int
    var nameV: string
    var iconV: string
    var resolved: string
    var item: int
    var pname: string
    var vh: int
    var neededMods: list of string
    var rtHeads: list of int
    var rtTail: int
    var rtPath: string
    var rtProbe: text
    var rtReadable: bool
    var i: int
    var j: int
    var h: int
    var beforeCount: int
    var beforeHits: int
    var hoists: list of int
    var hoistBefore: int

    // isUiProg reports whether this program declares any window, menu, or
    // `every` block -- computed from the checked AST decl list (`combined`)
    // so it's available BEFORE lowering: ir.cla's irIsUiProgram() reads IR
    // arrays lowerProgram doesn't populate until later, so it can't gate
    // the manifest splice below.
    isUiProg = false
    d = combined
    while d != -1 {
        if declKind(d) == DkWindow or declKind(d) == DkMenu or declKind(d) == DkEvery {
            isUiProg = true
        }
        d = declNext(d)
    }

    if want68k {
        d = combined
        appIdx = -1
        while d != -1 {
            if declKind(d) == DkApp { appIdx = d }
            d = declNext(d)
        }
        nameV = ""
        iconV = ""
        resolved = ""
        if appIdx != -1 {
            item = appDeclPropsHead(appIdx)
            while item != -1 {
                pname = poolGet(propertyName(item))
                vh = propertyValuesHead(item)
                if pname == "name" { nameV = poolGet(stringLitIdx(vh)) }
                if pname == "icon" { iconV = poolGet(stringLitIdx(vh)) }
                item = declNext(item)
            }
            if nameV != "" { resolved = nameV } else { resolved = poolGet(appDeclName(appIdx)) }
        }
        if resolved == "" { resolved = baseNameNoExt(entries[0]) }
        cgAppRawName = resolved
        if appIdx != -1 and iconV != "" {
            cgAppIconPath = joinPath(dirOf(poolGet(appDeclPathIdx(appIdx))), iconV)
        } else {
            cgAppIconPath = ""
        }
    }

    if not earlySpliceDone {
        cpCorePorted = true
        neededMods.add("core.cla")
        cpStrPorted = true
        neededMods.add("str.cla")
        cpTextPorted = true
        neededMods.add("text.cla")
        cpTextArcPorted = true
        cpListPorted = true
        neededMods.add("list.cla")
        cpListArcPorted = true
        cpMapPorted = true
        neededMods.add("map.cla")
        cpMapArcPorted = true
        if isUiProg {
            neededMods.add("uidesc.cla")
            neededMods.add("ui.cla")
            neededMods.add("uiwidgets.cla")
            neededMods.add("uitext.cla")
            neededMods.add("uitable.cla")
            neededMods.add("uiscript.cla")
            neededMods.add("uidialogs.cla")
        }
    }
    cpSerPorted = usesFileSaveLoad
    if usesFileSaveLoad {
        neededMods.add("ser.cla")
    }
    if want68k {
        neededMods.add("native.cla")
    }

    combined2 = combined
    if neededMods.count > 0 {
        if not haveRtDir {
            rtDir = findRtDir(neededMods[0])
        }
        hoistBefore = hoistCount
        i = 0
        while i < neededMods.count {
            rtPath = joinPath(rtDir, neededMods[i])
            rtProbe = ""
            rtReadable = file.readText(rtPath, rtProbe)
            if not rtReadable {
                if haveRtDir {
                    log("runtime module " + neededMods[i] + " not found (searched " + rtDir + "); use --rtdir")
                } else {
                    log("runtime module " + neededMods[i] + " not found (searched runtime/clarus/ from the working directory upward); use --rtdir")
                }
                quit 1
            }
            beforeCount = asmHeads.count
            beforeHits = dedupHits.count
            expand(rtPath, true, "")
            hoists = hoistDedups(beforeHits)
            j = 0
            while j < hoists.count {
                rtHeads.add(hoists[j])
                j = j + 1
            }
            j = beforeCount
            while j < asmHeads.count {
                if asmHeads[j] != -1 {
                    rtHeads.add(j)
                }
                j = j + 1
            }
            i = i + 1
        }

        // Rebuild the assembled program with the runtime modules' heads
        // FIRST (declare-before-use), followed by the original (already
        // fully linked) user-program chain. rtHeads holds asmHeads INDICES
        // (not heads), so each segment's own end comes from asmTails --
        // lastDecl would run off the end of a HOISTED segment, whose tail
        // Phase A already linked to the next user file's head.
        combined2 = -1
        rtTail = -1
        i = 0
        while i < rtHeads.count {
            h = asmHeads[rtHeads[i]]
            if combined2 == -1 {
                combined2 = h
            } else {
                rtTail = declListAppend(rtTail, h)
            }
            rtTail = asmTails[rtHeads[i]]
            i = i + 1
        }
        if combined != -1 {
            if combined2 == -1 {
                combined2 = combined
            } else if hoistCount == hoistBefore {
                rtTail = declListAppend(rtTail, combined)
            } else {
                // Hoisting happened: the user chain has to be re-linked
                // segment by segment, skipping the hoisted files (now in
                // the runtime chain above) and terminating explicitly --
                // the last kept segment's `next` still points at whatever
                // Phase A linked after it, which may be a hoisted file.
                i = 0
                while i < userHeadCount {
                    h = asmHeads[i]
                    if h != -1 and not hoistedHead[i] {
                        rtTail = declListAppend(rtTail, h)
                        rtTail = asmTails[i]
                    }
                    i = i + 1
                }
                rtTail = declListAppend(rtTail, -1)
            }
        }

        // checkProgram resets and rebuilds all check-global state itself
        // (the same reset+check sequence as the first pass). diags is
        // empty on entry (driveCompile's own first-check gate already
        // required that), so every diagnostic here is new.
        checkProgram(combined2)
        if diags.count > 0 {
            return false
        }
    }
    return true
}

// driveCompile runs the whole front-end-neutral pipeline: Phase A
// include-expand/lex/parse of every entry, the --testapi early splice, the
// first (and only, for a check-only/appinfo caller) checkProgram pass,
// then -- ONLY when the front end set wantEmit -- the runtime-manifest
// splice, a second checkProgram pass, and lowerProgram.
//
// Returns false on any diagnostic (or an unreadable entry file --
// entryFailed, checked by the front end BEFORE `diags`, exactly
// reproducing the old "cannot open entry file" message's precedence).
// Diagnostics stay in the existing `diags`/`diagPaths` stores exactly as
// before this task; driveCompile itself never prints or quits -- every
// front end renders them and decides its own exit code.
func driveCompile(entries: list of string, testapi: bool): bool {
    var i: int
    var h: int
    var tail: int
    var earlySpliceDone: bool

    i = 0
    while i < entries.count {
        expand(entries[i], true, "")
        i = i + 1
    }
    if entryFailed {
        return false
    }
    // Everything expanded so far is the user program's own; everything a
    // runtime-module splice expands from here on is runtime-side.
    userHeadCount = asmHeads.count
    i = 0
    while i < userHeadCount {
        hoistedHead.add(false)
        i = i + 1
    }

    // Assemble the post-order decl lists into one chain for the single
    // checker pass.
    combined = -1
    tail = -1
    i = 0
    while i < asmHeads.count {
        h = asmHeads[i]
        if h != -1 {
            if combined == -1 {
                combined = h
            } else {
                tail = declListAppend(tail, h)
            }
            tail = lastDecl(h)
        }
        i = i + 1
    }

    earlySpliceDone = driveEarlySplice(testapi)

    // Phase B: one checker pass over the whole program, collecting all
    // check-phase diagnostics (they follow every parse diagnostic).
    if combined != -1 {
        checkProgram(combined)
    }
    if diags.count > 0 {
        return false
    }

    if not wantEmit {
        return true
    }

    if not driveManifestSplice(entries, earlySpliceDone) {
        return false
    }

    irReset()
    lowerProgram(combined2)
    // native-gaps-cleanup Task 2: lowerProgram can itself emitDiag now
    // (lowCheckLiteral4CCArg/lowCheckLiteralFilter, lower.cla).
    if diags.count > 0 {
        return false
    }
    return true
}

// driveEmit68kFork (Task 9, mac-resident-clarusc) lowers the already-
// compiled program (driveCompile must have returned true first, with
// wantEmit/want68k set) all the way to a finished native 68k resource
// fork, WITHOUT the MacBinary wrap driveEmit68k below still does for the
// host `emit68k` CLI: roots the native-only entry points, synthesizes
// pascal glue for any callback, shakes, then hands off to cg68ProgramFork
// (cg68k.cla) -- the same segment-emission pipeline as driveEmit68k, just
// stopping short of the wrap+write. `nopeep` sets peep68k.cla's
// peepDisabled global, same as driveEmit68k always did. On success, the
// fork bytes/app name/creator are readable via driveLastFork/
// driveLastAppName/driveLastCreator below -- a front end that writes the
// raw fork itself (`file.writeRes`, Task 10) calls this instead of
// driveEmit68k and never sees a MacBinary byte.
func driveEmit68kFork(outPath: string, listing: bool, segLimit: int, nopeep: bool): bool {
    peepDisabled = nopeep
    // native-5d Task 7: root the native-only entry points BEFORE shaking.
    cg68AddRoots()
    // Task 8 (toolbox-integration Feature C): synthesize/root one pascal
    // glue IRFunc per `callback func` an ECbAddr decay was ever built for.
    cg68SynthCbGlue()
    shakeProgram()
    return cg68ProgramFork(outPath, listing, segLimit)
}

// driveEmit68k is the host `emit68k` entry point: driveEmit68kFork above,
// then the MacBinary wrap + write (cg68WriteImageWrap, cg68k.cla) --
// byte-identical to the old single-function driveEmit68k (which called
// cg68Program directly; cg68Program was itself exactly this same
// cg68ProgramFork-then-wrap sequence, now split across the two files at
// the fork seam instead of bundled in one cg68k.cla function).
func driveEmit68k(outPath: string, listing: bool, segLimit: int, nopeep: bool): bool {
    if not driveEmit68kFork(outPath, listing, segLimit, nopeep) {
        return false
    }
    return cg68WriteImageWrap(outPath)
}

// driveLastFork/driveLastAppName/driveLastCreator (Task 9, mac-resident-
// clarusc) expose driveEmit68kFork's last output to a resource-writing
// front end: the raw resource-fork image (no MacBinary header, no
// padding), the app's own declared name (appinfo naming rules -- the same
// name/appDeclName/entry-basename fallback `clarusc appinfo` uses,
// already resolved into cg68k.cla's cgAppRawName by driveManifestSplice
// above), and the four-char creator code cg68BuildFork resolved from the
// app section (declared app id, else "????"). Thin passthroughs onto
// cg68k.cla's own globals -- kept here, not there, since these are the
// documented front-end-facing contract surface (drive.cla owns the
// front-end-neutral pipeline; cg68k.cla owns codegen internals).
func driveLastFork(): text {
    return cgLastFork
}
func driveLastAppName(): string {
    return cgAppRawName
}
func driveLastCreator(): string {
    return cgLastCreator
}
